latest_plan = "Based on our reflection, here's a step-by-step plan to solve the challenge more effectively:\n\n1. Quadrant Analysis:\n   a. Divide the input grid into four quadrants.\n   b. Analyze each quadrant independently, identifying all non-empty regions.\n   c. For each region, record its color, shape, size, and position within the quadrant.\n\n2. Pattern Identification:\n   a. Compare the analyzed quadrants to identify similarities and differences.\n   b. Look for recurring shapes, color patterns, or positional relationships.\n   c. Identify any transformations between quadrants (rotations, reflections, color shifts).\n\n3. Transformation Rule Derivation:\n   a. Based on the comparisons, formulate a set of rules that describe how patterns transform across quadrants.\n   b. Consider rotations (90°, 180°, 270°), reflections (horizontal, vertical), and potential color changes.\n   c. Account for possible pattern evolution or growth between quadrants.\n\n4. Central Cross Analysis:\n   a. Examine the central cross (rows and columns 14 and 15) in the input.\n   b. Determine if the cross is part of the overall pattern or should remain black.\n   c. If part of the pattern, include it in the transformation rules.\n\n5. Empty Quadrant Prediction:\n   a. For any empty quadrants, use the derived transformation rules to predict their content.\n   b. Apply the rules to the known patterns from non-empty quadrants.\n\n6. Pattern Application:\n   a. Create a new 30x30 grid for the output.\n   b. Apply the identified patterns and transformation rules to fill each quadrant.\n   c. Ensure the central cross is handled according to the analysis in step 4.\n\n7. Consistency Check:\n   a. After filling the grid, check for overall symmetry and consistency.\n   b. Verify that the transformation rules have been applied correctly across all quadrants.\n\n8. Refinement:\n   a. If inconsistencies are found, refine the transformation rules and reapply.\n   b. Iterate on steps 5-7 until a consistent, symmetric result is achieved.\n\n9. Final Validation:\n   a. Compare the generated output with the input to ensure all original patterns are preserved or transformed according to the derived rules.\n   b. Verify that the output maintains the essential characteristics of the input while achieving the desired symmetry.\n\n10. Output Generation:\n    a. Once validated, return the final transformed grid as the solution.\n\nThis plan allows for a more flexible and comprehensive approach to pattern recognition and transformation. It can handle complex cases where simple mirroring or rotation is insufficient, and it considers the possibility of pattern evolution and color transformations. The iterative nature of the plan also allows for refinement of the rules if initial attempts don't produce a satisfactory result."
